findings.body:
The base code clamped the semver-derived code to `[1, 2100000000]` in dev mode *before* the `== 0` and `> 2100000000` checks, so a `0.0.0` version in dev became `1` and built fine. This PR moved the clamp *after* the checks. Under `tauri android dev`, `0.0.0` (the default many projects still have) now bails with "You must change the `version`...", and an oversized version errors instead of being clamped.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/android/mod.rs" lines="652-665"}

The dev clamp should run first so dev builds stay permissive, as they were before.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/android/mod.rs" lines="652-665"}
if dev {
  version_code = version_code.clamp(1, 2100000000);
}

if version_code == 0 {
  crate::error::bail!(
    "You must change the `version` in `tauri.conf.json`. The default value `0.0.0` is not allowed for Android package and must be at least `0.0.1`."
  );
} else if version_code > 2100000000 {
  crate::error::bail!(
    "Invalid version code {}. Version code must be between 1 and 2100000000. You must change the `version` in `tauri.conf.json`.",
    version_code
  );
}

The only place that wrote `app/tauri.properties` on every cargo build was `tauri-build`, which ran for any build that set `TAURI_ANDROID_PROJECT_PATH`. That writer is removed here, and the replacement only runs from the CLI's `build` and `dev` commands. The Android Studio path (`tauri android android-studio-script`, in `android_studio_script.rs`) calls `target.build(...)` directly and never calls `generate_tauri_properties`. So a user who changes `version` in `tauri.conf.json` and then builds from Android Studio or Gradle gets a stale `versionName`/`versionCode`. On a fresh checkout, Gradle falls back to `1` and `1.0`.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-build/src/mobile.rs" lines="10-11"}

Either keep the `tauri.properties` write in `tauri-build` (computing the same value from the config it already receives) or call `generate_tauri_properties` from the Android Studio script before the cargo build. Also call this out in the changefile, since it is a user-visible behavior change.

When `autoIncrementVersionCode` is true, the code reads the last versionCode from `app/tauri.properties`, and on any failure (missing file, unreadable file, non-`u32` value, a key with spaces around `=`) `.unwrap_or(1)` silently writes `versionCode=1`. The template `app/.gitignore` ignores `/tauri.properties` by default, so a fresh clone or CI checkout will always restart at `1`. Google Play rejects uploads whose versionCode does not increase, so this produces a hard-to-diagnose upload failure rather than a clear build error.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/android/mod.rs" lines="635-646"}

When the file exists but yields no valid value, fail loudly. When the file is absent, the least surprising behavior is to surface a warning or error that tells the user to commit `tauri.properties`, not to silently use `1`.

The increment happens in `generate_tauri_properties`, which `dev.rs` calls on every `tauri android dev` session. Because the feature requires committing `tauri.properties` (per the schema doc), each dev session leaves a modified tracked file in the working tree. The increment also runs before `build::setup` and the cargo build, so a build that later fails still consumes a versionCode. Neither behavior is documented, and both create churn in version control and gaps in versionCode.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/android/dev.rs" lines="274-274"}

Consider incrementing only for release `build` (where `dev` is false), or making the increment opt-in per command, and documenting the dev behavior. At minimum, state in the schema doc that every `dev` run also increments the value.

The PR adds a non-trivial function with several branches: the auto-increment read/parse/increment, the precedence between auto-increment, explicit `version_code`, and semver-derived logic, the `0.0.0` and overflow errors, the dev clamp, and the write-if-changed check. None of it has test coverage. The base repo has no tests for the removed `tauri-build` path either, so the behavior changes above would not be caught by CI.

::code{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/android/mod.rs" lines="625-646"}

Add unit tests for `generate_tauri_properties` against a temporary project directory. Cover: auto-increment from an existing file, first run with no file, an unparseable value, precedence over `version_code`, dev vs release for `0.0.0`, and the overflow boundary.

The reader matches `line.starts_with("tauri.android.versionCode=")` on the raw line, with no trimming and no handling of Java properties syntax. A hand-edited file with leading whitespace or `tauri.android.versionCode = 5` is not matched, so the code falls back to `1`. The `split('=').nth(1)` step also drops everything after a second `=`. Gradle's `Properties.load` accepts all of these forms.

::code{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/android/mod.rs" lines="638-644"}

Use `line.trim()` and `strip_prefix` on the key, and split on the first `=` only, so the reader accepts the same syntax Gradle does.

The explicit `version_code` and semver paths are bounded to `1..=2100000000`, and the schema enforces the same range. The auto-increment path uses `saturating_add(1)` on a `u32` and then writes whatever comes out. Once the stored value reaches 2100000000, the next build writes 2100000001, or `u32::MAX` if the value is already at the top, instead of failing with the clear message the other paths give.

::code{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/android/mod.rs" lines="645-646"}

Apply the same upper-bound check used in the semver branch to the incremented value, and report a clear error when the limit is reached.

When `autoIncrementVersionCode` is true, the `else if` chain never reads `bundle.android.versionCode`. A user who sets both gets the auto-incremented value with no warning, and the explicit value is discarded. The doc comment on the new field says it falls back to `version_code` only when the flag is false or unset, which supports this precedence, but nothing tells the user their explicit value is being ignored.

::code{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/android/mod.rs" lines="635-648"}

Either emit a warning when both are set, or reject the combination in config validation, so the precedence is visible rather than silent.

The changefile lists `tauri-build` as `minor:feat` and describes only the new config option. This PR also removes `tauri.properties` generation from `tauri-build` and changes dev-mode version validation in the CLI. Neither is mentioned, so release notes will not tell users that Android Studio and Gradle builds stop refreshing versionName/versionCode, or that `tauri android dev` now behaves differently for `0.0.0` (see the ordering finding).

::diff{file="/.changes/auto-increment-android-version-code.md" lines="1-8"}

Update the changefile text to describe the removed `tauri-build` behavior and any dev-mode validation change, or split the removal into its own changefile entry once the regressions are fixed.
